From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Economic Law (Not Suggestions!)

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

   This is the nature of spontaneous order. Imagine a group of humans
   each with unique resources dropped on an island. The probability
   that the resources have been distributed in the optimal manner is
   very low. Naturally some of those humans desire the resources of
   some of the others.  There are two natural ways the system alters
   itself. Either someone gets a big stick, clubs a guy over the head
   and takes his possessions, or they decide to trade.  (the other
   option, called "sharing" doesn't seem to work except in small
   families, and even then, conflicts continually occur).

There seems to be considerable evidence that in relatively static, pre-urban
societies (even today), that "sharing" systems have worked over large
communities.  For more details, see the references I posted yesterday.

Yes, conflicts do occur, but there are various methods for
adjudicating conflicts.  (No different from any other society.)

The big disadvantage of such a system is that it is tradition-bound,
and so can't cope with rapid change.

As a footnote, it seems like a lot of the collectivists want to
re-create such a system.  Much Communist rhetoric has that flavor, and
"Stranger In A Strange Land" is quite explicit about it.

Dale

