Or, should anarchocapitalists support laws against "fraud"?

(This is a big topic, one which I've been itching to get into for a
long time. I'll just make a few comments for now.)

A few months back, around the time of the U.S. election, someone here
was commenting that candidates who call themselves "libertarians," but
who are not (in some sense, or members of the LP), should be _sued for
fraud_. I was very uncomfortable with this notion.

The recent thread about Geller, Randi, and magic raises the issue
again. Is magic a fraud? Are the gullible deserving of protection?
(And, if so, by whom?)

Realize that if fraud laws are actually enforced, then essentially all
religions (or "N - 1" of them, since at most one of them can be proven
to be "true," however unlikely this is!) will get shut down. Ditto for
so many other areas that I cannot begin to list them here!

(Ironically, this ties in with some of the concepts of "proof"
discussed in connection with PPL on this list, and with some of the
Heidegger debate of several weeks ago, which I won't go into here.)

If some bozo makes outrageous claims, whether for some miracle diet or
for some religious salvation plan, I think of "evolution in action" (a
fool born every minute, don't believe everything you're told, and
never buy software from a guy named Bill).

The trends in our society toward "protecting" people by passing all
kinds of laws about truthfullness, fraud, etc., are incredibly
statist. (How long will cryonics last in this kind of society?)

I'm not sure how a PPL system would handle this. I suppose people
could contract with agencies to "shield them" from the likes of
religious zealots and magicians, to screen their phone calls, and to
review all contacts. Sort of like what religions now do for their
members. No harm done.


Sandy Sandfort raises this issue of "fraud" in connection with some
comments made by (I believe) Owen Rowley:

