I see no crime here. If this kind of "fraud" is halted by the state,
say goodbye to nearly every freedom you can imagine. Religions will
go, health claims will go, and so will any "unproven" claims or opinions.


......
> featured on "Lifestyles of the Rich and Famous."  This crook is
> doing a lot more than simply entertaining.  Uri has a company
> which charges corporations tens-of-thousands of dollars to give
> them advice based on his "psychic powers."

Sounds fair to me! Any company that would hire psychics, dowsers,
spiritualists, Baptists, Yogis, or Extropians deserves whatever
happens to it.

Written, enforceable _contracts_ are another matter altogether (though
I lean heavily toward the notion of PPL markets for measuring
enforceability, perhaps via some of Robin Hanson's techniques). For
example, a written contract to find water at a site is easily
enforeable: if water is found, payment is made...else, no payment.
(This is easily handled through third-party escrow services). The
issue of whether a water dowser is a fraud is never a legal issue.

The whole "fraud" and "proof" issue is a very slippery slope. I urge
folks to consider free market alternatives, such as the common sense
rules we grew with, before proposing to "outlaw" fakes like Geller or
charlatans like L. Ron Hubbard.

