Date: Wed, 27 Jan 1993 22:03:00 -0500
From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: A couple of stray points on vegetarian diets.

1) It is probably the most valuable to compare the SAD diet (Standard
American) with a healthy vegetarian diet, which involves a number of
interrelated changes. Just removing meat from a white-bread-and-sugar
diet isn't the same as learning to eat a variety of whole grains and
whole vegetables (and what WAS in that "all-liquid" test diet??)
  For one thing, although plant foods *are* lower in some nutrients,
the volume of food consumed increases on a vegetarian diet. If you
can, find some tables that rank *nutrients* vs *calories* and you will
see that the amount of nutrients consumed by the vegan can be quite
high.
  For another thing, the body's requirements can change with diet.
Our RDA's were calculated by testing people eating the high-protein
high-fat diet of Americans in the middle of the century. Some studies
show that, for example, our need for calcium goes way down as protein
consumption decreases.
  Also, and this relates also to the eskimo, natural selection tends
to favor any trait that keeps an individual alive long enough to
reproduce. Most of the problems with a high-fat high-meat diet are
things that show up after the reproductive years; for women, some of
these problems occur mainly after menopause. Osteoporosis and coronary
artery disease are killers of older adults; I for one wish to be a
healthy older adult.
  By the way, the Inuit eat a diet quite different from ours; they eat
the ENTIRE animal, organs, marrow, fish bones, and the entire stomach
contents of their catch. Their diet has the highest intake of calcium
and protein of any people in the world and they have one of the
highest rates of osteoporosis in the world.

(Mazes, R, "Bone Mineral Content of North Alaskan Eskimos," Journal
 of Clinical Nutrition, 27:916, 1974, cited in Robbin's Diet for a New
America, p.194. This book has *great* footnotes)


[I have heard that the Eskimo also show two evolutionary differences
